# Web structure (`apps/web/src/`)

Mirror the API: features live in `domains/<domain>/`, everything else is shared shell.

- `domains/<domain>/` — one folder per feature (auth, tenant, account, task, images, billing, marketing, admin):
  - `api.ts` — typed calls for this domain (wraps `@/lib/api`); the only place that builds this domain's routes
  - `context/` — React context for this domain when needed (create with the first provider; e.g. auth, tenant). File: `context/<name>-provider.tsx` co-locates Provider + Context + `useX` hook
  - `hooks/` — reusable domain hooks that are not just the context consumer (create with the first hook)
  - `pages/XxxPage.tsx` — route-level screens (PascalCase, one per file). Non-page route elements (e.g. redirects) may omit the `Page` suffix
  - `routes.tsx` — the domain's routes; **every domain with pages has one** (see routing below)
  - `components/` — domain UI (kebab-case): selects, FABs, layouts, marketing mocks… Create with the first non-page component; do not leave domain UI loose at the domain root
  - A domain may import another domain's `api`/context/components, but keep it minimal
  - Do not create empty `context/` / `hooks/` / `components/` folders — add the folder when the first file lands
  - Public marketing screens (landing, pricing…) live in `domains/marketing/`. The landing page
    (`LandingPage.tsx`) embeds code snippets that mirror the repo's real conventions (repository,
    route, tool, page template) — if a convention changes, update those snippets too
  - Platform admin (`domains/admin/`) mounts at `/admin` outside `:tenantSlug` — gated by `me.user.isPlatformAdmin`
  - AI spend UI lives under Billing; the API `usage` domain is ledger-only (no web mirror)
- `app/` — application shell: `App.tsx` (route map), `config.ts` (`useAppConfig()` → `selfSignupEnabled`, `aiEnabled` — the server-driven flags), `NotFoundPage.tsx`
- `layouts/` — AppLayout (`Shell()` sidebar `items` for primary nav; account/billing/integrations live in the user-menu dropdown), AuthLayout, RequireAuth
- `theme/` — the theming system: `themes.ts` (each theme = light + dark token sets; add a brand by copying a block and changing `--primary`), `theme-provider.tsx`, `theme-controls.tsx` (includes the language picker). Components never hardcode colors — they use the CSS variables
- `i18n/` — UI locales: `index.ts` (i18next init), `locales/en.json` + `pt.json` (app shell) and `landing.en.json` + `landing.pt.json` (public landing). User-facing copy goes through `t("…")` — add keys to both locale files of the matching pair
- `lib/` — pure infra: `api.ts` (raw HTTP client + `ApiError` + `showApiError`), `utils.ts` (`initials`, `formatUsdMicros`); no domain knowledge
- Network calls go through a domain `api.ts`, never `fetch` or the raw HTTP helpers on `@/lib/api` from a page. Pages may still import `ApiError` / `showApiError` from `@/lib/api`
- Imports: `@/` for anything crossing a domain or into shell/`lib`/`theme`/`i18n`; relative (`./`, `../`) only within the same domain (same rule as the API). Import base UI from `@app/ui`. There is no top-level `src/components/` — product UI lives in the owning domain's `components/`

## Shared UI package (`@app/ui` → `packages/ui`)

Framework-agnostic, tenant-agnostic base UI shared across web apps. Import per component via subpath:
`import { Button } from "@app/ui/button"`.

- Primitives (shadcn — don't hand-rewrite): `avatar`, `badge`, `button`, `card`, `dialog`,
  `dropdown-menu`, `input`, `label`, `resizable`, `textarea`
- Generic composites: `confirm-dialog` (`ConfirmProvider`/`useConfirm`), `empty-state`,
  `page-header`, `page-loading`
- `cn` lives here: `import { cn } from "@app/ui/lib/utils"`
- Nothing in `@app/ui` may import from `@/` or reference a domain — keep it app-neutral. Anything
  with product copy or business logic stays in `domains/<domain>/components/` instead.

## Page template (follow it for every internal page)

Reads use `useQuery`; writes use `useMutation` and invalidate on success. No manual `useState`
loading flags for data. Copy `domains/task/pages/TasksPage.tsx` — it is the canonical example.

- Reads: `useQuery({ queryKey: [resource, tenant.id], queryFn: () => api.list(tenant.id) })`
- Writes: `useMutation({ mutationFn, onSuccess: invalidate, onError: (e) => showApiError(e, "...") })`
- Open with `<PageHeader title description />`; render `<PageLoading />` while `isLoading`; render
  `<EmptyState>` when a list is empty
- Destructive actions use `const confirm = useConfirm()` → `if (await confirm({ title, destructive: true }))`, never `window.confirm`
- Errors from mutations always go through `showApiError(err, fallback)`

## Reusing UI — reach for these before writing new UI

From `@app/ui`: `PageHeader`, `PageLoading`, `EmptyState`, and `useConfirm`/`ConfirmProvider`
(`@app/ui/confirm-dialog`). Domain UI: e.g. `RoleSelect` (`domains/tenant/components/role-select`).
If a generic UI shape repeats across pages, add it to `@app/ui`; if it carries product copy/logic,
keep it in the owning domain's `components/`.

## Routing

Each domain exports its routes from `routes.tsx`. Top-level route groups (auth, marketing, tenant, admin)
are composed in `app/App.tsx`. Domains rendered **inside the tenant app shell** (task, images, billing, account)
export their `<Route>` and are composed inside `tenant/routes.tsx` under the `:tenantSlug` layout.
Sidebar nav is the `items` array in `Shell()` (`layouts/AppLayout.tsx`); account/billing go in the user menu.
